SAU language reference
======================

SAU (Scriptable AUdio) is a simple and non-Turing-complete
language for mathematical audio synthesis,
without support for the use of pre-recorded samples.

The core idea of the language is that of time-ordered steps for
configuring audio generation: add an oscillator, then later,
change a parameter and extend play duration, etc. A script is
basically a list of such timed instructions. Language constructs
also offer more flexible arrangement of steps to take than a
bare flat list of instructions and forward time movement.

The syntax is a bit terse and unusual. It uses one-character
keywords with or without an argument, followed by zero or more
parameters with arguments. Each main keyword provides an action,
either at run time (like an object constructor in other languages),
or at parse time (like a global script setting).

Some upper-case letters are type names for signal generators,
and are used to add instances of them -- respectively:
 - A: Amplitude generator
 - N: Noise generator
 - R: Rumble oscillator
 - W: Wave oscillator
Such objects can be connected for modulation. Together with the timing
logic and parameters, these are the core features of the language.

Keyword parameters may either be set (by writing the name,
followed by immediately writing their argument(s)), or left out
to use the previous value or a default value. Some default values
can be changed using the S (Script options) parse-time action.

Modifiers are somewhat like keywords, but use another character
than a letter as a name. They are used alongside the keywords to
adjust time placement. They are simpler and more flexible in how
they may be used.

Scopes
------

The flow of time and the nesting of scopes are like two dimensions
in which things are arranged. For altering the time-arrangement,
see 'Timing'; that includes substeps, which are considered part
of the same larger step involving the same reference to an object.

Beginning a new step, by introducing a new object or new reference
to an object, terminates the previous when done in the same scope.

The global level of a script is a top scope containing objects and
steps for them. Each list ("[...]") is a similar subscope. What is
written for an object inside a list is a step in the inner scope,
with timing connected to the outer scope. Termination of an inner
step does not terminate any outer it is related to (i.e. nesting).

Keywords
--------

Each of these keywords is further described in its own section below.
	S	Script options (parameter default value or other);
		runs during parsing. Changes made inside a
		nested list scope don't apply outside of it.
	A	Amplitude generator -- "A", optionally followed by
		amplitude values as for "a", e.g. "A1.0", "A0[...]".
	N	Noise generator -- "N", optionally followed by
		the initial "Noise type" value, e.g. "Nre".
	R	Rumble oscillator -- "R", optionally followed
		by the initial "Line types" value, e.g. "Rlin".
	W	Wave oscillator -- "W", optionally followed
		by the initial "Wave types" value, e.g. "Wtri".

S: Script options
-----------------

Set parameter default value or other option; runs during parsing.
Changes made inside a nested list scope don't apply outside of it.

Usage: "S", followed by zero or more whitespace-separated parameters,
each with a value.

Check the descriptions of the corresponding actual parameters rather than
the below default values or modifiers for them for more information on how
parameters work. Below the focus is on the script options handling.

Parameters:
	a	Multiplier for amplitude "a" values after, in the current
		scope of "[]" nesting. Starts at 1.0. The multiplier also
		applies to the second value for value range modulation, that
		for envelopes, and to the multiplier inside any AM modulator
		list except the range-mapping ".r[]" list. It does not apply
		to other nested lists for modulators, that is modulators for
		other parameters; their multipliers apply unchanged.
		.f	Script-wide mix frequency filter controls.
			See "Frequency filter options" for details.
		.m	Script-wide gain mix control (multiplier). If used,
			this disables automatic down-scaling of amplitude
			by the number of voices (max carrier audio generators
			simultaneously producing signals), for manual control
			instead. Can only be set in the outermost scope.
	c	Default channel mixing "c" value. Starts at 0.0,
		i.e. C (center). Useful as a main way of setting
		the parameter value.
		.p	Default pan law. One of 'l' (linear, center -6 dB,
			default), or 'f' (full, center -0 dB).
	e	Envelope setting. To change the envelope subparameter for
		several parameters, in any generators after in the same scope
		or a nested list scope. After setting options here, to apply
		them to an envelope subparameter afterward, it must be named
		in the script, for example "f.e" names a generator's frequency
		envelope. Options for "S e" are the same as those in any ".e"
		subparameter, except for a lack of sweep and modulator values.
			See "Value ranges and envelopes" for more.
	f	Default frequency "f" value, in Hz. Starts at 440.
		.k	Key selection for "f" values using note syntax
			("Frequencies as notes"), default C4. For a just
			intonation (JI), the note for this key will use the
			first ratio in the scale. Also changes the default
			and relative octave. Can be C, D, E, F, G, A, or B,
			with or without one of 'b'/'f' (flat), 's' (sharp),
			'd' (half-flat), 'z' (half-sharp),
			'v' (flat-and-a-half), 'k' (sharp-and-a-half),
			'w' (double-flat), or 'x' (double-sharp) --
			and/or an octave number (0-10) to move the default
			from a 4-5 range to one of the number to the number
			plus one.
		.n	A4 tuning frequency in Hz for "f" values
			using note syntax. Starts at 440.
			For example, use "S f.n432" for 432 Hz.
		.s	Tuning system, either 'e' (24-EDO, default),
			'p' (Pythagorean JI), 'c' (classic 5-limit JI),
			or 'j' (SAU 7-limit JI).
	r	Default relative frequency "r" value, a modulator:carrier
		ratio. Starts at 1, meaning 1/1 times the carrier's frequency
		parameter is used. (For an n:m ratio, use a n/m value.)
			This is the frequency default used for modulators
		which have a carrier at some level with a frequency
		parameter, rather than the "f" value default.
	t	Default short definite time "t" value, in seconds, also
		named as T. Default times may be longer (and occasionally
		shorter) depending on the context. Starts at 1.0.

Signal generator common parameters
----------------------------------

Generator universal parameters:
	Named parameters common to all generator types --
	"A", "N", "R", and "W".
	a	Amplitude, where 1.0 corresponds to a level of 0 dB and
		0.0 is silence. (Note that the "S a" option can be used to
		set a multiplier for this value. Further options affect mixing
		and the final output level afterwards; this includes panning,
		which may reduce output level unless fully left or right.)
		Can be negative to flip the sign of the signal.
			"Value ranges and envelopes" is supported for AM &
		RM (amplitude and ring modulation); see section.
		.f	Generator output frequency filter controls.
			See "Frequency filter options" for details.
	c	(For non-nested generators only.) Channel mixing, mainly
		(-1.0) to 1.0. See "Channel mixing values" for more.
			"Value ranges and envelopes" is supported for
		panning-AM (stereo placement amplitude modulation); see
		section. The result of modulation is added to the channel
		mixing used. (If you downmix the result to mono and use
		the default linear panning, the effect will disappear,
		provided there's no clipping etc. first.)
		.p	Pan law or mode. One of 'l' (linear, center -6 dB,
			default), or 'f' (full, center -0 dB).
	f	Frequency in Hz. Does nothing in a non-oscillator, yet is
		provided for all generator types, to control pitch in
		nested oscillators from a wrapper generator.
			See "Oscillator common parameters" for more.
	r	(Only for generators nested inside a carrier.) Relative
		frequency. Does nothing in a non-oscillator, yet is
		provided for all generator types, to control pitch in
		nested oscillators from a wrapper generator.
			See "Oscillator common parameters" for more.
	t	Time duration in seconds. If no "t" setting is given, the
		time set depends on the context.
			For a single non-nested generator, 1.0 is used unless
		the default value is changed with "S t".
			When several generators are specified, the default
		time is based on the longest remaining (at the current time)
		duration of play in use for any step at the current level,
		in considering the surrounding sequence of steps and delays
		(up to the next '|' time separator, or to the end of the
		script if none).
			The default time lengthens further when modulators
		with longer definite times are specified for the current step.
			For modulator generators, default time is however an
		"implicit" time length (see 'i', "Time values"), meaning
		playing whenever a carrier it is linked to does. (Implicit
		time is only supported for nested generators.) When such a
		time length is retrieved as part of setting the default time
		for something else, a definite default time in seconds (e.g.
		1.0 seconds or "S t") is however used instead.
			For steps began with a "@name" label reference or a
		":name" label copy, the default time (aside from modulator
		implicit time) is the last time got through the label. That's
		the time for the last prior "@name" reference if any, or if
		none, the object when and where the "'name " label was added.
			For a compound step, the first substep uses "S t" for
		the default time, unless it used a label to get an object.
		The following substeps in turn each have the time of the
		previous as its default time. The exception is modulators,
		for which the last substep has implicit time ('i', "ti")
		by default, just like for undivided steps for modulators.
			See "Time values" for named constants, etc.

Oscillator common parameters:
	Named parameters common to types "R" and "W".
	They also have the "Generator universal parameters".
	f	Frequency in Hz. Can be negative to flip wave shape timewise.
			"Value ranges and envelopes" is supported for FM
		(frequency modulation); see section. Note that the modulator
		lists for "f" and "r" expressions are shared and identical.
			Note that for FM modulators under this "f" or "r",
		the carrier frequency used as a multiplier is simply the
		unmodulated value. (If several types of FM list are set and
		used at the same time, the result of earlier stages will be
		used for later.) For other kinds of modulation, if FM is also
		done its result is used.
	r	(Only for generators nested inside a carrier.) Relative
		frequency, a value which will be multiplied by the frequency
		of a selected carrier which has a frequency parameter in the
		chain for the modulator, to give the frequency to use. For an
		n:m modulator:carrier frequency ratio, a value of the form
		n/m may be used; e.g., for a 4:3 frequency ratio, "r4/3".
			"Value ranges and envelopes" is supported for FM
		(frequency modulation); see section. Note that the modulator
		lists for "f" and "r" expressions are shared and identical.
			Under "r" the same values are set as under "f", the
		difference simply being whether multiplication by carrier
		frequency is switched on or off. Specific values like the
		main paramater value or the value range ".r" second value can
		be set again under "f" or "r" to toggle just that value.
		Sweeps ("Value sweep") can also go from an "f" state to an
		"r" goal, or from an "r" state to an "f" goal; the goal
		decides the behavior after the first value.
			The following subparameter can be used to change the
		target carrier whose frequency multiplies values under `r`.
		The default is the carrier just above, the immediate
		container of this modulator.
		.c	Ratio carrier, as an integer indexing the modulator
			list nesting level. The default is -1 (as in "r.c-1"),
			negative values going up a number of steps from this
			modulator. A positive value or 0 will count up from 0,
			the root/top, towards this modulator. The selection is
			clamped to the range of root carrier to carrier just
			above, no matter the numbers entered.
	p	Phase in percentage of the wave cycle modulo 1.0. Set to
		reset the phase, e.g. to change the initial value from 0.0.
		See "Cycle position values" for more.
			"Modulator list" values are supported for PM (phase
		modulation); see section. A phase percentage value can be set
		together with a list in one go, the value then going first
		after the 'p', for example "p0.25[...]".
			A subparameter can be used instead or in addition
		to set up another kind of PM. Phase distortion can also be
		used; see the "Phase distortion subparameters" section for
		those options. There you'll also find the "p[a]" self-PM
		a.k.a.  "feedback FM" subparameter described.
		.f	Frequency-scaled PM modulators can be set in a
			separate list, as in "p.f[...]". See "Modulator list".
		The sum of all PM modulator amplitudes will phase-modulate
		the carrier(s). For frequency-scaled PM modulators, first the
		amplitudes are multiplied by the carrier frequency divided
		by 632.45... Hz (the geometric mean of 20 and 20000 Hz).

Phase distortion subparameters:
	These phase "p" subparameters can be written within a "p[]" list,
	as heading subparameters. This can also be used after the oscillator
	type names "W" and "R" (since they accept "p" values as a shorthand
	syntax), e.g. "W[c2.f2 d2]" or "Wsqr[x3/4]".
		The phase distortions below are performed in the listed
	order. Self-PM a.k.a. "feedback FM" is listed among them as it can
	be used in the same places (and likewise distorts waveform through
	distorting phase) though it isn't conventionally grouped under
	the same heading. The PD options allow modulator lists, and non-LFO
	modulation of PD variations give spectrally richer alternatives to
	PM that behave differently.
		"Value ranges and envelopes" (and in turn "Value sweep"
	and "Modulation with value ranges") is supported for all of these;
	see the section for more.
	c	Cycle length or "zoom out" PD; the default value 1.0
		does nothing. Values above 1 "zoom out", e.g. 2 will
		double the cycle length, so the old shape then takes
		up the first half with horizontal line padding after
		(the line having the cycle boundary amplitude). With
		a value below 1, the effect is to "zoom in", such as
		reducing a sine to the first quarter with 1/4.
			Negative values work like positive ones, but
		also flip the direction of the waveform, in the same
		way as changing the sign of the frequency used does.
			Amounts to a basic form of Pulsar Synthesis;
		waveforms for "zoom in" differ, and further options,
		for PS-specific waveform shaping, are missing.
			Using modulators with audible frequency for
		this value will often sound somewhat like PM but bright
		and buzzier.
	d	Duty cycle or "zoom in" PD; the default value of 1.0
		does nothing, values close to 0 "zoom out" and above
		1 values "zoom in". This is the multiplicative inverse
		of "c", but both are set and applied independently.
		It's the "PulWM" of Pulsar Synthesis. Compared to "c",
		this is the option allowing a full "zoom out", instead of
		a full "zoom in". When sweeping or modulating one of
		them, the part of the range close to 0 is that which
		will audibly come and go quickly.
			Using modulators with audible frequency for this
		value will sound somewhat like doing it with "c", except for
		being both more deep and brightly buzzy. It has the richest
		sound on being modulated compared to the other PD types.
	h	Hold for cycle portion phase distortion; the default
		value 0.0 does nothing. A positive value like 1/4 is
		used to extend a horizontal line from the beginning,
		overwriting e.g. 1/4 of the cycle. A negative value,
		by contrast, extends the line from the end backward,
		so that -1/4 overwrites the last 1/4 of the cycle.
			Using modulators with audible frequency for this
		value will often sound a little like PM, but much thinner
		and sharper -- and large amplitudes result in silence.
	x	An x-axis half-cycle length PWM-like PD. Generalized
		PWM (pulse-width modulation) in this way makes pulse
		waves out of square waves. It makes other wave types
		take on related shapes too, stretching/squeezing the
		waveform around its "center" in a cycle. The default
		value 1/2 does nothing. 0 shrinks to nothing the 1st
		waveform half, 1 the 2nd; values beyond these bounds
		are allowed, creating different distorted shapes.
			Using modulators with audible frequency for this
		value will often sound somewhat like PM but warmer, fat,
		and buzzy. This and "d" both tend to sound deep when
		modulated, but this sounds less bright.
	y	Inverse of the x-axis half-cycle lenght PWM-like PD,
		"x". Where PWM changes length proportions of halves
		of a waveform while preserving their shapes, instead
		this distortion deforms one half, by reducing change
		in phase inside it, the other half being accelerated
		and displaced to compensate. Default 1/2, meaning no
		change, with 0 and 1 extremes -- with values allowed
		beyond as well, creating different distorted shapes.
			The effect is identical to that of using a PM
		modulator which is a triangle wave (especially with
		frequency ratio 1:1). Using modulators with audible
		frequency for this value corresponds to PM nested a level
		deeper, under such a modulator. It will often sound
		somewhat like less-nested PM but brighter, yet smooth.
	a	Phase self-modulation (a.k.a. "feedback FM") can be set
		using a value, which is used as a multiplier for adding
		the previous amplitude back into the phase. Non-zero values
		add sawtooth-like harmonics, morphing the waveform in that
		manner. Positive values make for an increasing sawtooth
		shape, negative values make it a decreasing sawtooth.
			Going beyond +/- 0.5 may add ripples, ringing,
		and finally noise and chaos. (1.0 corresponds to Yamaha
		DX7 and chips level 6, but filtering differs so it's less
		noisy. Double or halve the number to step up or down by one
		in Yamaha's level 1-7 scale. In relation to how self-PM is
		usually mathematically described, the value 1.0 translates
		into the formula's modulation index at pi radians.)
			When combined with more modulation, self-PM is most
		useful for innermost PM or similar modulators, which can
		have frequency ratio etc. changed to vary what it causes.
		For example, "W[W[a1/2] r2]" gives a soft square-like wave
		because of the 2:1 modulator:carrier frequency ratio.
	The following subparameters are available for all PD options, i.e.
	all but the self-PM "a" option.
	.f	Subfrequency, default 1. For "c" and "d", this is a
		frequency multiplier for the non-padding part of the
		waveform, applied independently for each cycle. For the
		other PD types, it instead subdivides the cycle into a
		number of regions, the distortion being applied to each;
		using an even integer for the value will make the
		distortion produce odd harmonics only.
			Non-integers cause discontinuities.
	.p	Phase offset for the cycle boundary position. This rotates
		where the distortion is applied. See "Cycle position values"
		for more. (This parameter differs in that values reaching
		integer multiples of 1.0 shift seed position for the "R"
		oscillator, much like strong PM inputs.)

Seedable common parameters:
	Named parameters common to all seedable types,
	those with randomizable results -- "N" and "R".
	They also have the "Generator universal parameters".
	s	Seed as percentage of the state space, i.e. from 0.0 to 1.0,
		accepting any value taken modulo 1.0. This overrides the
		default seeds given to new instances, which are randomized
		using a sequence based on the global "$seed" magic variable.
		See "Cycle position values" for more.

A: Amplitude generator
----------------------

Generates sweepable amplitude offsets, i.e. DC offsets.
Can also be used for mixing, as "A0[...]" produces the sum
of the "modulators" and nothing else (though the modulators
are technically AM, added under amplitude). Likewise,
"Modulation with value ranges" can be used to multiply AM
inputs. Instances allow greater mixing flexibility inside
modulation lists of either type, belonging to any object.
	It's also possible to use it with an amplitude
envelope in order to produce a periodic waveform, using
the loop envelope mode "el", as in "A1.e-1[el ...]".

Usage "A", optionally followed directly by amplitude values
used by the "a" parameter (which for convenience can be set
directly for "A", e.g. "A1.0", "A0[...]").

Followed by zero or more whitespace-separated parameters, each
with a value.

Parameters:
	The "A" amplitude generator has the
	"Generator universal parameters".

N: Noise generator
------------------

A plain noise generator, which outputs noise sample pairs at Nyquist
frequency, producing a selected noise color and distribution.

As a signal generator, if not enclosed within a "[]" list, then
it will run and output at the current time, for its duration.

Note that non-white noise has a sample-rate dependent sound, when
generated at maximum rate as with this generator. For consistent
noise at a per-sound rate of your choice, other means are needed.
A violet noise that always peaks at a frequency of your choice can use
the "R" oscillator with mode "mv", and the chosen frequency set to "f".

Usage "N", optionally followed by a noise type (e.g. "Nre", if none by
by default the same as "Nwh"), and optionally followed by amplitude "a"
values. For example "Nwh(1/2)" (equivalent to "Nwh a1/2"), and "N(0)[R]"
(equivalent to "N a0[R]").

Followed by zero or more whitespace-separated parameters, each
with a value. See "Noise types" for the available noise types.

Parameters:
	The "N" noise generator has the
	"Seedable common parameters", and additionally:
	n	Noise type -- see "Noise types" for values.

Noise types:
	wh	Uniform white noise.
	gw	Gaussian white noise, soft-saturated.
	bw	Binary white noise.
	tw	Ternary smooth white noise. Every other sample is zero.
	re	Red/brown noise based on uniform white noise.
	vi	Violet noise based on uniform white noise.
	bv	Binary violet noise. Differentiated 'tw' noise.

R: Rumble oscillator
--------------------

Rumble oscillator, a.k.a. random line segments oscillator.
An oscillator for both value noise and 1D Perlin noise; it
connects pseudo-random values generated at a dynamically variable
frequency with line segments of a selected shape. Two random values and
line segments are generated each "cycle", so that the base frequency
matches that of a wave oscillator -- though the spectrum otherwise differs
in most modes. Fully supports FM & PM like a wave oscillator does.

As a signal generator, if not enclosed within a "[]" list, then
it will run and output at the current time, for its duration.

Usage: "R", optionally followed by a line type (e.g. "Rlin", if none by
default the same as "Rcos"), and optionally followed by phase "p" values
(number and/or modulators) and a subparameter (including but not limited
to "Phase distortion subparameters"). For example "R[W]" (equivalent to
"R p[W]"), and "Rcos(-1/4)[W]" (equivalent to "Rcos p-1/4[W]").

Followed by zero or more whitespace-separated parameters, each with a
value. Several 'm' modes are available, for several types of random
distribution, as well as a naive oscillator mode. See "Line types" for
the available line types. The default mode and line shape makes
the output similar to low-pass-filtered white noise.

Parameters:
	The "R" rumble oscillator has the "Oscillator common parameters",
	"Seedable common parameters", and additionally:
	l	Line type -- see "Line types" for values.
	m	Mode for line start and goal value variation; consists of
		a letter (random function) and a digit (0-9 shaping level);
		one or both may be set at a time; the default level is '9'.
		Roughly, each level above 0 halves what remains of the
		unshaped underlying randomness. The functions are...
		u	Uniform random (default). Ignores the level setting.
		g	Gaussian random, soft-saturated approximation. On
			average ~6 dB quieter. Ignores the level setting.
		b	Binary random. Extreme levels, more repetitive runs.
		t	Ternary smooth random. Never repeats twice in a row;
			cycles above or below zero, randomly flips polarity.
		f	Fixed cycle. Plain naive oscillator at the top level;
			below it, mixed with randomness at reduced amplitude.
		a	Additive recurrence low-discrepancy a.k.a. quasirandom
			sequence. Ignores level digit. Uses subparameter ".a".
		In addition to the function and level, these flags can be set.
		h	Half-shape waveform. Use with 'lin' for a decreasing
			sawtooth instead of a triangle wave; similarly changes
			the shape for all line types and randomness modes.
		p	Perlin noise mode. This reshapes the waveform so it
			returns to 0 at each cycle boundary. This removes
			the lowest frequencies, and shifts the peak of the
			frequency spectrum to the base frequency. Can be
			combined with any noise function and other mode flags,
			including violet noise modes for extra-thin noise.
			Transforms binary noise into a smoother noise. The
			amplitude may drop for ternary smooth random mode 't'.
		s	Square, then restore sign, of the start/goal values.
			Turns uniform value variation into uniform energy
			variation; somewhat quieter, and more tremulant.
			Doesn't affect 'b', 't', nor 'f' with level '9'.
			Distorts 'v' violet noise toward white, as if mixed.
		v	Violet rather than white noise version of the function
			if available; for 'u', 'b', and 'f', missing for
			'g', 't', and 'a'. Like high-pass filtering
			the lower end of the noise, 6 dB per octave.
		z	Zig-zag flip. Swap ends of each half-cycle, adding an
			inharmonic waveform jaggedness unless using 'h', or
			'f' level '9'; more difference from these adds larger
			sharp steps. Always flips the waveform top and bottom.
		There also exists these subparameters used for specific modes:
		.a	Multiplier constant used for additive recurrence mode,
			defaulting to "met(1)". Only the fractional value part
			is significant and used. Note that values like 0.0 and
			1.0 result in silence (or DC offset); using irrational
			numbers gives good results and random ones often work.

Line types:
	cos
		Half cosine (S-curve) trajectory over time.
	lin
		Linear trajectory over time.
	sah
		Sample and hold until time (then jump to goal).
	exp
		Scaled "exp(6x)-1"-like increase or decrease.
	exp11
		Scaled "exp(11x)-1"-like increase or decrease.
	log
		Scaled "log(6x+1)"-like increase or decrease.
	log11
		Scaled "log(11x+1)"-like increase or decrease.
	xpe
		Exponential shape (saturate or decay), steep as 'exp'.
	xpe11
		Exponential shape (saturate or decay), steep as 'exp11'.
	lge
		Logarithmic shape (saturate or decay), steep as 'log'.
	lge11
		Logarithmic shape (saturate or decay), steep as 'log11'.
	sqe
		Square polynomial envelope (saturate or decay).
	cub
		Cubic polynomial segment (-1 to +1) trajectory.
		Spiky ends, the opposite of a sinuous shape.
	smo
		Smoothstep (degree 5). A sinuous curve adding
		mild odd harmonics like a soft-clipped 'cos'.
		Traditionally used for Perlin noise.
	ncl
		Noise camel line; softer, two noise bulges.
	nhl
		Noise hump line; harder, one broad noise bulge.
	uwh
		Uniform random white noise in start-goal range.
	The 'xpe' shape was originally mainly for frequency sweeps;
	it may be too soft for some uses. For percussive use, 'xpe11'
	is normally sharp enough. For amplitude sharping, a less steep
	shape may be needed; 'sqe' is way softer than 'xpe'.

W: Wave oscillator
------------------

Wave oscillator. The sine variety is a fairly typical "FM synth operator".
By default (see the 'm' parameter) it produces a (weakly) anti-aliased
signal, including for FM/PM.

As a signal generator, if not enclosed within a "[]" list, then
it will run and output at the current time, for its duration.

Usage: "W", optionally followed by a wave type (e.g. "Wtri", if none by
default the same as "Wsin"), and optionally followed by phase "p" values
(number and/or modulators) and a subparameter (including but not limited
to "Phase distortion subparameters"). For example "W[W]" (equivalent to
"W p[W]"), and "Wsin(1/4)[W]" (equivalent to "Wsin p1/4[W]").

Followed by zero or more whitespace-separated parameters, each with
a value. See "Wave types" for the available wave types.

Parameters:
	The "W" wave oscillator has the
	"Oscillator common parameters", and additionally:
	m	Mode for further behavior variation.
		A letter (implementation type) below can be set...
		a	Antiderivative anti-aliasing (default). Weakly
			anti-aliased result for any combination of wave
			type, FM, PM, and phase distortion -- but may give
			unwanted results for some PD variations. Dampens
			frequencies close to half the sample rate a little.
		n	Naive. Gives the most classic "FM operator" behavior.
	w	Wave type -- see "Wave types" for values.

Wave types:
	Beyond 'sin', there's 3 times 3 complementary wave types, in terms
	of the added harmonics (odd, even, or all), and mellow vs. bright.
	Additionally, there's 2 more, listed after these first 10.
	sin
		Sine. For cosine, set phase 'p' to 1/4.
	tri
		Triangle.
		Mellow odd-harmonics wave.
		Opposite of 'ean' relative to 'par'.
	srs
		Square root of sine. (Mirrored for the negative half.)
		Medium-bright odd-harmonics wave.
		Opposite of 'cat' relative to 'mto'.
	sqr
		Square.
		Bright odd-harmonics wave.
		Opposite of 'eto' relative to 'saw'.
	ean
		Evenangle.
		Mellow even-harmonics wave.
		Opposite of 'tri' relative to 'par'.
		To begin at 0.0 amplitude, set phase 'p' to 6/93.
	cat
		Catear.
		Medium-bright even-harmonics wave.
		Opposite of 'srs' relative to 'mto'.
		To begin at 0.0 amplitude, set phase 'p' to 1/16.
	eto
		Eventooth.
		Bright even-harmonics wave.
		Opposite of 'sqr' relative to 'saw'.
	par
		Parabola. (x^2, steep part up.)
		Mellow all-harmonics wave.
		Between 'tri' and 'ean'.
		To begin at 0.0 amplitude, set phase 'p' to 9/87.
	mto
		Mellowtooth. (Half-rectified 'srs', amplitude doubled.)
		Medium-bright all-harmonics wave.
		Between 'srs' and 'cat'.
		To begin at 0.0 amplitude, set phase 'p' to 1/25.
	saw
		Sawtooth.
		Bright all-harmonics wave.
		Decreasing slope; use negative amplitude
		or frequency (but not both) for increasing slope.
		Between 'sqr' and 'eto'.
	hsi
		Half-rectified sine. (Amplitude doubled.)
		Like a somewhat louder 'ean', harmonics decreasing as fast.
		To begin at 0.0 amplitude, set phase 'p' to 1/12.
	spa
		Sine parabola. (First half, amplitude doubled.)
		Slightly cleaner than 'par'. Mainly useful for modulation.
		To begin at 0.0 amplitude, set phase 'p' to -1/12.

Values and expressions
----------------------

Whitespace is not allowed within multi-character names, keywords or
numbers, and separates values. Spaces and tabs may otherwise be used or
omitted anywhere.

Comment syntax:
	"//" (C++-comment) comments out the rest of the line.
	"/*" (C-comment) comments out text until the next "*/". Does not nest.
	"#!" (Shebang) comments out the rest of the line.
	"#Q" (Quit file) comments out the rest of the whole file.

Numerical expressions:
	A number can be specified with or without a decimal point;
	for a number with a decimal point, a leading zero can be omitted.
	Number signs and arithmetic operation symbols can be used in infix
	expressions, together with numbers and named constants, variables,
	and functions. But unless something is written within parentheses,
	it cannot contain any whitespace, as it ends the expression. For
	example, "-1" is fine, but "- 1" is a dangling minus followed by
	a dangling number 1, if not inside parentheses as "(- 1)".
		The following operations are recognized, and grouped below by
	priority (nested parentheses can be used to change evaluation order):
		^	To the power of (right-associative)
		* / %	Multiplication, division, remainder
		+ -	Addition, subtraction
	Parentheses also allow shorthand multiplication (leaving out a
	"*" between two parts), e.g. "2(3)" and "(2)3" both give "6".
		Some parameters support named constants specific to that type
	of value, like "Frequencies as notes", "Channel mixing values", and
	others. Such a name can be written instead of a number.
		The following universal mathematical symbols (functions and
	constants) can also be used in any numerical expression; functions
	require parentheses after the name (and most often require a value
	inside), while constants are simply written as names:
		abs(x)	Absolute value of `x`.
		arbf(x)	Additive recurrence base frequency. Returns a
			multiplier from -1 to +1 for how much the pitch will
			change for an "R" instance when `x` is set as the
			value for "R ma.a". May be negative, corresponding to
			direction in a sawtooth-like wave which rises rather
			than falls; negative frequencies are supported.
		arhf(x)	Additive recurrence higher frequency. Returns a
			multiplier from -2 to -1 or +1 to +2, locating the
			first frequency above the unshifted base frequency,
			with a value which mirrors arbf(x) around +/- 1.0
			with the same sign.
		cos(x)	Cosine of `x`.
		exp(x)	Base-e exponential value of `x`.
		log(x)	Natural logarithmic value of `x`.
		met(x)	Metallic value, e.g. "met(1)" gives the golden ratio.
			Positive integers give the series of metallic ratios.
			Other values are also allowed: fractional, 0 giving 1
			and negative (gives how much the positive value would
			be increased, approaching zero further from zero).
			Note that met(-x) is also equal to (1/met(x)).
		mf	632.45... Geometric mean of 20 and 20000. Middle
			frequency in Hz of nominal human hearing range.
		pi	3.1415...
		rand()	Pseudo-random number in range 0-1. The PRNG is seeded
			using the magic variable, "$seed". The value sequence
			from a series of calls restarts each new script.
		rint(x)	Round value `x` to the nearest integer. Halfway cases
			are rounded to the nearest even integer.
		sin(x)	Sine of `x`.
		sgn(x)	The sign of `x` as +/- 1 or 0.
			(The sign bit is also preserved for 0.)
		sqrt(x)	Square root of `x`.
		time()	Get a system timestamp number changed each second.
			It can be used for seeding in a randomized script.
			(Note that the exact value is platform-dependent.)
			If disabled (deterministic mode), instead gives 0.

Frequency filter options:
	For filter ".f" parameters such as "a.f". Low-pass filter (LPF),
	high-pass filter (HPF), or both, can be used. The following named
	options can be set inside the ".f[]" list as heading subparameters.
	l	LPF cutoff (-3 dB) frequency in Hz, or 0 to disable the
		filter. The filter used is a plain 1-pole LPF.
	h	HPF cutoff (-3 dB) frequency in Hz, or 0 to disable the
		filter. The filter used is a plain 1-pole HPF.
	There's also a shorthand syntax for using only at most one filter
	at a time. Writing a number directly after ".f" will set: LPF only,
	if the number is positive; HPF only, using the negation of the
	number if it's negative; or neither if the number is 0.

Channel mixing values:
	Panning, where 0.0 is centered. Named constants can be used in place
	of numbers for the three classic channel "modes". Values outside the
	range of L to R are allowed, giving one channel a negative amplitude
	while possibly amplifying the other (depends on selected pan law).
	C	0.0
	L	(-1.0)
	R	1.0

Cycle position values:
	Phase offset as a percentage of the wave cycle, or seed position as a
	percentage of the state space. Any value will be used modulo 1.0. For
	example, for "p" the value 1/4 turns sine into cosine.
		Named constants provide scaled angles which can be used in
	expressions, e.g. (G*n) for some whole number n makes for the nth
	leaf-around-a-stem angle.
	G	0.3819... (golden angle as cycle percentage)

Time values:
	Constants for time in seconds. There is also the non-number
	literal 'i' for implicit time, valid to set to time "t" for
	modulators only.
	T	The value corresponding to the "S t" default duration,
		which in turn by default is 1 second.

Value ranges and envelopes:
	Various parameters support dynamic changes to their values. Apart
	from the basic plugging in of modulators (see "Modulator list"),
	there's also sweeps (see "Value sweep"), envelopes -- described in
	more detail below -- and "Modulation with value ranges".
		These features can be combined. A sweep is a one-off timed
	gradual change for a value, often available for a subparameter along
	with the option of a modulator list to mix a signal with its value.
	An envelope uses a range of values with two bounds, with parameters
	to set up a repeating pattern of change. Value ranges can also be
	used with modulators to pick values in a (possibly dynamic) bounds.
		At the language level, the sweep is the lowest-level thing
	that happens first. Thereafter, a value range for modulation can
	enter, having two bounds (and possibly two sweeps), and various
	modulator lists. Finally, an envelope if used is applied on top of
	the result of that; it uses that intermediate result as one boundary
	for another value range, and has settings for another one (which can
	be swept and modulated) along with special envelope parameters.
		The envelope has timed ADSR stages (attack, decay, sustain,
	and release), which rotate and each adjust the resulting level in
	sequence. It begins with the attack stage when "t" (time, among
	"Generator universal parameters") is set for the generator which has
	the envelope. When the envelope reaches the end of the release stage,
	the "t" time will have elapsed. In-between, the sustain stage takes
	up however much time you get, if any, when subtracting the ADR times
	from the time set to the generator "t" parameter.
		If the envelope belongs to a modulator which has implicit
	time ("ti", the default), then it will base its timing on the time
	set for the carrier instead. (That is, the nearest carrier, going
	outward from the modulator -- however many levels of nesting until
	a definite "t" time is found.)
		Note that the ';' substep separator timing syntax, if used,
	always implicitly sets a new "t" value for a generator after the end
	of the old, and therefore triggers a new use of envelopes. This makes
	it easy to make a sequence of sounds using envelopes. If instead you
	want to adjust a generator after a time without re-running envelopes,
	you can use an alternative timing syntax (see "Timing modifiers").
		Envelope subparameters for ADSR etc. can be set inside an
	envelope ".e[]" list, where ".e" is in turn a main envelope
	subparameter for parameters. Values can also be set by first using
	the "S e" option to set them there, then naming each envelope for any
	generators (for example "a.e" for the amplitude envelope) to apply
	them to. A value can be overridden in each envelope by setting it
	there, beyond just naming the envelope.
		Where parameters take a main value, the ".e" subparameter
	takes a second value, similarly to the ".r" short-form syntax. Either
	can be written either in a short form just after the main value, or
	in a long form following the long-form ".." (see "Modulation with
	value ranges"). Again, the ".r" and ".e" second values are separate,
	and ".e" envelope processing runs after ".r" range-mapping.
		Note that when the short-form ".r" or ".e" are used, the
	meaning of setting modulators for the main value just before will
	change; such list changes are then moved to the list corresponding
	to the long-form final mix addition modulators, ".a[]" after "..".
		The envelope treats the second value as the internal "low"
	level before attack and after release (usually the "bottom" for an
	amplitude envelope) and the main value as the "high" level between
	attack and decay. (This is the other way around compared to ".r".)
	a	Attack time (default 0 seconds). If above zero, the result
		will "fade in" towards the main value.
	d	Decay time (default 0 seconds). If above zero, the result
		will take time moving to the sustain level (or staying there
		if it's 1.0),
	s	Sustain level (default 1.0). If not equal to one, the decay
		stage will change the level. 1.0 is mapped to the main value
		and 0.0 to the second value.
			Values outside the range of 0.0 to 1.0 are allowed.
		If below 0.0, the level goes beyond the second value, and the
		release then returns to it the other way around. If above 1.0,
		the level goes beyond the main value, like a second attack.
	r	Release time (default 0 seconds). If above zero, the result
		will "fade out" from the sustain level to the second value.
			The special non-number literal 's' can also be set,
		to "stretch" the release to fill all available time, making
		it replace and so remove the S (sustain) stage. The sustain
		setting then still applies for setting the level from which
		the release stage starts.
	e	Envelope settings. A letter or digit can be set to toggle
		behavior for the envelope.
		0	Off. The envelope does nothing.
		c	Clip envelope shape. If the current generator "t"
			time linked to the envelope is too short for all
			stages, cut off the too-long portion. Release, and
			maybe earlier stages, may go unfinished before any
			retriggering and abrupt level jump to a next attack.
		d	Declicked envelope shape (default). Like clip mode
			'c', except that if time runs out before completion,
			the last value reached is kept and the next attack
			is changed to start from it. (If a stage of 0 time
			length is reached, it is always completed.)
		l	Loop envelope. This changes the behavior into that of
			a periodic waveform generator. Attack immediately
			follows release, and there's no sustain time. In
			terms of timing, the envelope runs like a modulator.
		s	Shrink stage times. To avoid clipping the envelope
			shape, each time the envelope is triggered, adjust
			its stages to fit their combined duration to the
			linked generator time "t" value. By reducing times,
			this lets the envelope always "finish".
				Release time is reduced first, then the times
			of earlier stages if still needed.
		The following 'e' subparameters exist.
		.l	Set line type for every stage at once. If set at the
			same time as the line type for a specific stage,
			the stage-specific setting overrides this.
	Each of 'a', 'd' and 'r' have the following subparameters.
	.l	Line fill shape (default 'lin'); see "Line types".

Value sweep:
	To sweep a parameter which supports sweep subparameters towards a
	goal value -- the ordinary value being the start for a trajectory --
	following the ordinary value or by itself, the following value sweep
	subparameters can be given values prior to any objects within a new
	"[]" list. For example, "f[g220]" sweeps frequency to 220 Hz over a
	default time.
	g	Goal (go-to) value, assigned to the parameter after time.
		This value has no default and must be provided. If changed
		again before the full time, the current point reached on the
		previous trajectory will be used to change the start value.
	l	Line fill shape (default 'lin', or the previous shape if any)
		-- see "Line types" for values.
	t	Time to reach goal (default is the external "t" duration,
		or the remaining previous time, if any, for this parameter).
		If longer than the active time for the object which has the
		swept parameter, the trajectory will be left unfinished.
	v	Start (state) value, the ordinary parameter value.
		It can alternatively be set here after a 'v',
		if not set before the enclosing "[]".

Modulator list:
	Within "[]", written after the name of a parameter that supports it,
	signal generators can be included for use with that parameter. For
	example, for PM the phase parameter 'p' is assigned a list as in
	"p[...]". It works the same for other parameters such as 'a'
	(for AM/RM) and 'f' (for FM). There's also further options and
	subparameters for modulation, see "Modulation with value ranges",
	which all build on the use of the modulator list.
		Assigning a list to a parameter will append the new list to
	any old one, expanding it rather than replacing the old items. Thus
	"p[]" changes nothing. To clear old items when setting a list, add
	'-' before the '[', as in "p-[]" (which removes all PM modulators).
		Lists can be assigned together with other values (numbers)
	for various parameters. For the whole expression beginning with the
	parameter name, whitespace can only be placed inside list brackets
	(or inside parentheses for any numerical expressions).
		"Value sweep" for a parameter can use the same list as one
	which contains modulators; the modulators simply need to be listed
	after any sweep subparameters (see section) which head the list.
	The parameter phase "p" instead supports other heading subparameters
	inside a list (see "Phase distortion subparameters").
		It's also possible to write multiple lists directly after
	one another when setting to a parameter (with or without the one
	leading '-'). These lists will be joined into one, meaning that
	"[X][Y]" for some contents "X" and "Y" is the same as "[X Y]".
	This also allows placing heading subparameters in a later list.

Modulation with value ranges:
	This is one of the value range features common to all parameters
	supporting "Value ranges and envelopes", apart from the similar
	envelopes that can be used at the same time, and the "Value sweep".
		For every parameter supporting this, the result of modulation
	is used in place of the main value for the parameter. For amplitude,
	whether the result is called amplitude modulation (AM), or ring
	modulation (RM), depends on how carrier and modulator amplitude are
	set up relative to one another. (For frequency modulation, the result
	is however always the "real FM" related to yet distinct from PM,
	whenever modulation happens.)
		Following a parameter name and optionally its main value(s),
	modulators can be set in a list (see "Modulator list") to mix their
	output into the main value. (The result may be more complex if more
	options are used along with this.) For example "a0[...]" will set
	amplitude to 0, and the modulators within "[...]" will have
	the effect of ring modulating, while with "a1" set the result is AM.
		Subparameters can be used for greater flexibility, to set up
	a value range with two bounds (the main value for the parameter, and
	a second value playing the part of the other bound). Another set of
	modulators, called "range mapping" modulators, are then used to pick
	values in the range. There's both a long-form and a short-form syntax
	for this, where the long form allows modulator lists which add things
	to all of the main value, the second value, and to the final result
	after using the two values with the range mapping modulators.
		An example of how to vary frequency with the short form is
	"f200.r(200 * 2)[...]", where the expression for ".r" is the second
	value and the list after contains the range mapping modulators.
	The description below begins with the long-form "..", which sets it
	apart from the short form.
		The second value always defaults to 0.0, and "Value sweep"
	is always supported for both it and the first value.
	..	The "name" for the subparameter where the second value goes
		for the other end of the range. A number and/or modulator
		list after can optionally be set here; any modulators have
		their output applied to the second value, shifting that
		endpoint of the range around. (In turn the second endpoint is
		used only when range mapping modulators bring it into use.)
			After "..", the following options can be used -- their
		use can be concatenated in the order listed -- with any left
		out. For example, just ".r", ".e", or ".a" -- or ".r.e".
		.r	The long-form range mapping subparameter, which sets
			the mapping to the range, modulator outputs used to
			pick values in it. It only accepts a modulator list
			(or nothing). This modulator list works as described
			below for ".r" (the short-form parameter).
		.e	Envelope options; see "Value ranges and envelopes".
			This is the second value for an envelope; envelope
			processing runs after ".r" value range mapping, so
			the result of ".r" becomes the first value for the
			envelope.
		.a	The final mix addition subparameter, which takes
			modulators whose output is added to the result after
			value range mapping and any envelope processing.
				The short-form ".r" (see below) and ".e" (see
			"Value ranges and envelopes") parameters, when used,
			move this option to the modulator list placement for
			the main value, as a shortcut for accessing this.
	.r	The short-form range mapping subparameter, which accepts the
		second value, and a list of modulators whose outputs are
		used to pick values in the range. The value is optional, as
		is setting a list. (If there's nothing used in this list,
		mapping is stuck at the main value only.)
			When ".r" is written just after a main value modulator
		list, this changes the meaning of that list. Changes to it are
		then considered to be changes to the ".a" final mix addition
		in the long-form syntax, nested under ".." -- as a shortcut
		for tweaking that instead of main value endpoint modulators.
			Range mapping modulators have their results combined
		differently from other modulator lists. Each modulator here
		produces a result in the range of 0.0 to 1.0 (i.e. a positive
		signal) multiplied by its amplitude (with a default of 1.0),
		instead of -1.0 to +1.0. But a negative amplitude setting for
		a modulator likewise flips the top and bottom of the output
		range (0.0 and 1.0 are swapped, the signal remains positive).
			When several modulators are used here, their outputs
		are multiplied, producing a result biased towards the 0.0 end
		of the range, with broader valleys and narrower peaks. This
		product of ithe outputs is then mapped to a range where 0.0
		is matched to the main value, and 1.0 matched to the second.
			However, changing amplitude settings for modulators
		can shift the end of the range corresponding to the second
		value. If the internal 1.0 becomes bigger, the range grows,
		and if smaller, the range shrinks.
			To use this for classic 100% modulation depth AM,
		one of the bounds should be 0.0 (like the default for the
		second value); while for classic RM, the two bounds should
		have the same magnitude, but with opposite sign.
			Note that AM done inside, attached to a modulator's
		"a" (amplitude) parameter using any syntax, may be distorted.
		Internal mixing follows a rule of "y=(x*a + abs(a))/2", where
		`x` is a generator's main signal and `a` is everything coming
		from the amplitude parameter. This is specific to mixing in
		".r" lists. (For a constant or LFO `a` this will sound clean.
		If `a` has a higher frequency it may be audibly half-wave
		rectified, full-wave rectified, or some blend of the two.)

Variables:
	Variables hold numbers and can be used in numerical expressions.
	They are lexically scoped relative to lists (see "Modulator list")
	and can be assigned to several times (including using the previous
	value on the right-hand side of '=').
		To assign to a local variable, "$name=" can be written,
	immediately followed by a numerical expression. The name is
	case-sensitive and can have any sequence of alphanumeric characters
	and/or '_'. Once assigned, "$name" can be used in any numerical
	expression after. (The leading '$' sets variables apart from other
	numerical names.) Whitespace is allowed both before the '=' and
	after it (before the numerical expression after).
		To get/set values from/to a global variable from any lexical
	scope, "$~name" can be used. Otherwise, though readable with "$name"
	if there's no local variable of the same name, assigning to the name
	without using "$~name" will create a new local variable.
		A non-overriding numerical assignment (mostly useful for
	passing options to a script) uses '?=' in place of '='. Such an
	assignment is done only if the variable didn't already hold a number,
	otherwise nothing happens (and the expression after '?=' is skipped).
	If used in a nested scope, it checks for the variable at any scope.
		At the global scope, a '?' can instead be written before a
	variable name, "$?name=", to warn if it wasn't already set, while
	"$?name" by itself instead makes passing a value a hard requirement,
	the script not running unless "$name" is set before the "$?name".
		Numerical expressions for some named parameters can use
	context-sensitive constants; to also allow their use when assigning a
	variable, one of the below parameter namespace names can be added
	after the '='. Between it and any number or mathematical name after
	must be whitespace and/or a mathematical symbol, to keep names apart.
	For example, "$freq=f A4" has the frequency value of the note "A4".
	c	Channel mixing values
	f	Frequencies as notes
	p	Cycle position values (phase)
	s	Cycle position values (seed)
	t	Time values
		Built-in magic variables exist that perform a procedure when
	set, beyond holding a value. Like other variables, the initial values
	can be changed by passing options to a script to assign variables.
	To get/set these special variables from a non-global list scope, use
	the "$~name~ syntax (see above).
		$seed	Set to reset the rand() value sequence; defaults to 0
			if no value was passed to the script. Does not hold a
			number when checked unless a value was passed or set,
			allowing non-overriding assignments, like "$seed?=1".
			Keeps the last value set; "$seed=$seed" later resets.
			(Every bit counts; different expressions for the same
			number, with e.g. rounding may give different seeds.)

Labels for objects:
	To label an object, "'name " can be written just before it is added
	or referenced. The name is case-sensitive and can have any sequence
	of alphanumeric characters and/or '_'. The name can then be used to
	refer back to the object using either syntax below, to start a new
	step anywhere later in the script for the object or for a copy of it.
	:name   Copy a named object and insert the copy for use in the
		current nesting scope. The copy will inherit the state
		of the copied object at the time of the copy step. All
		modulator objects are also cloned, once per copy step.
	@name   Refer back to an original named object and reconfigure it.
		An "@name" step differs from other steps for an object in
		not automatically setting a new time duration for the
		object. Use "t" (see "Parameters") or other time-altering
		syntax (see "Timing") to change the generator's time
		duration value, e.g. to restart or stop it.
			Does not move the original object from its old
		nesting scope, nor into that where the "@name" reference
		is located. The object is treated as if the reference had
		been made in the original nesting scope for the object.
		The time scope is however new and of the reference.

Frequencies as notes:
	Frequency values may be specified as notes, using symbolic named
	constants. These can be either MIDI note numbers ("M0" to "M127"
	where "M69" by default is 440 Hz), or C-D-E-F-G-A-B notes placed in
	some octave from 0 to 10. The "S f.n" option sets the A4 tuning
	frequency, by default 440 Hz. The tuning system can also be changed
	using "S f.s", to use just intonation instead of equal temperament.
		By default, notes use the 24-tone equal temperament or equal
	divisons of the octave (24-EDO) system, a superset of 12-EDO. There
	are also three justly intoned systems (Pythagorean JI, classic
	5-limit JI, SAU 7-limit JI), in all of which each of the 7 notes
	have a natural, 3 flat, and 3 sharp variations, all of them unique.
		For MIDI note numbers, each number which doesn't correspond
	to some natural C-D-E-F-G-A-B note (i.e. 1, 3, 6, 8, and 10, for
	some note number modulo 12) is given a value exactly between the
	surrounding two. (For JI this will differ both from the flat note for
	the number above and from the sharp note from the number below, both
	of which will also differ. For equal temperament all are the same.)
	Note suffixes for flats and sharps (including microtonal ones) can
	be used as for C-D-E-F-G-A-B notes.
		Each named note is written with a C, D, E, F, G, A, or B. As a
	first optional suffix, a 'b'/'f' (flat) or 's' (sharp) can be added --
	or a quartertone alternative: 'd' (half-flat), 'z' (half-sharp),
	'v' (flat-and-a-half), or 'k' (sharp-and-a-half). There's also
	'w' (double-flat) and 'x' (double-sharp). The flats and sharps
	differ for EDO and the three JI systems -- making a smaller
	difference in the JI systems than for EDO, except for the
	Pythagorean JI which very slighly exaggerates it instead.
		An octave number (0-10) can be written as a final suffix,
	e.g. "A5" matches twice the tuning frequency, "Ad5" a little below
	that. With no number, the octave for the note will be relative to the
	key setting ("S f.k"), and by default 4-5, so that the note set for
	the selected key is also the lowest using the low default octave.
	To access more octaves from a relative position, add arithmetic.
		Another means of microtonal variation is a subnote prefix
	for "inner octave" placement, optionally added first: c, d, e, f,
	g, a, or b. (It can be combined with any other options.) The result
	is moving the tone, part of the distance from the diatonic note used
	to that above it. I.e., "cC" is the same as only "C", but "dC" moves
	up one subnote step towards "D", "eC" another step, and so on. (What
	about "eCs"? It likewise moves two subnote steps from "Cs" towards
	"Ds", as the 's' is applied separately.) If the diatonic part of the
	key selected is not C, this small letter scale rotates with it. In
	just intonation, the frequency increases apply rational fractions.

Timing
------

Two concepts of timing are at play in a script:
 * Static timing i.e. parse-time timing doesn't require audio generators to
   actually run. It is time according to how things are placed in a script.
   The timing modifiers are used to affect static timing. Parameter sweeps
   (see "Value sweep") also work according to static timing; a sweep always
   moves forward, even if what is swept is a parameter for a paused or
   unused audio generator.
 * Dynamic timing i.e. process-bound timing depends on how something inside
   a script runs, like how the time for a modulator only advances when the
   carrier runs, the modulator otherwise being paused. Subcomponents of an
   audio generator generally only advance when it does. For example, an
   amplitude envelope is paused, and phase in an oscillator does not change
   according to the frequency, when the oscillator does not run.

Timing modifiers:
	|	Time separator. Adjusts delay added to everything after, to
		ensure it is as long as the remaining duration (play time)
		of what comes before, increasing it otherwise. Does not
		decrease added delay.
	/number	Forward shift, time in seconds. Delay the next step and
		all steps placed after. The next step can be either a
		split-out continuation of the current step, or new.
			Does not automatically extend time duration on
		splitting a step, unlike ';'.

Compound steps:
	For a step written for some object, timing can be changed locally,
	within only the step and for lists nested under it. Two varieties
	of the ';' substep separator allow this. Their use can be repeated.
	;	The numberless ';' step split can be written after a step for
		an object (on a new line or the same), to specify a new time
		duration and new parameter arguments which apply just after
		the previous time duration. The new duration generally has
		the length of the previous by default. (For the default time
		of the first substep, and special handling for the last, see
		"t" under each "Parameters" for more).
			The time handling is designed to simplify writing a
		sequence of connected, non-overlapping timed updates for a
		single object. For example (three frequencies, one a second):
		"Wsin f100 t1; f200; f300". Changing "t1" in this example
		changes the time length for all three parts.
			For more flexibility, especially for adding "silent"
		gaps between parts, the numberless ';' can be combined with,
		or replaced by, the ';number' gapshift. Combination is easy,
		as "; ;number" will subdivide and shift the second substep
		by "number" of seconds, and move the active time duration for
		the second substep past the second split, creating a gap
		"number" of seconds long. For example (1 second of silence
		between frequency changes): "Wsin f100 t1;;1 f200;;1 f300".
	;number	Gapshift, time in seconds. When a number immediately follows
		the ';', then the new substep is placed in time that number
		of seconds after the previous, instead of after the duration
		of the previous. Depending on usage, may move, alternatively
		extend, the current sound in time.
			For ease of adding silent time padding, before the
		";number" part (but not after it) the default time duration
		is changed to 0, so that any time value automatically set
		there will be 0. After the ";number" part, a time value is
		always set, the last "t" or default (before zeroing) value.
			Several uses of ";number" to separate substeps in
		a row (no numberless ';' in-between!) will only zero the
		default time before the first ";number", allowing e.g. to
		extend the whole time rather than to move it by adding a
		leading ";0" before another ";number".
